                    briefing_text_r1:       `Resist the instinct to read high hours as the disease. Using the Vogel & Bruch matrix, locate the division by judging <strong>intensity</strong> (high/low) and <strong>quality</strong> (positive/negative) <em>separately</em>. The data mixes signals on purpose: 9.2 overtime hours and long circular meetings say high intensity — but cynicism, blame and post-merger friction say negative quality. That combination is <strong>corrosive energy</strong>, not exhaustion-from-passion and not simple burnout. The trap is treating the exhaustion as the disease rather than the symptom of a relationship and autonomy problem the reorganisation created.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the destination & shortlist your levers`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Set the target state — <strong>productive energy</strong> means high intensity AND positive quality, not just calmer or just harder. Then decide which mobilising strategy you lead with: <strong>Slaying the Dragon</strong> (rally against a shared threat, fastest for corrosive energy) or <strong>Winning the Princess</strong> (a compelling vision, best for sustaining). Run the acceleration-trap check — are you de-loading or piling on more? — and shortlist how many levers you will personally sponsor. Leadership attention, not cash, is the scarcest resource: two levers done with conviction beat five that fade by week six.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence & commit exactly two levers`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The constraint bites: <strong>£600,000</strong> and two levers' worth of leadership attention. Pick <strong>two</strong> levers and <strong>sequence</strong> them across the 12 weeks. Order matters — launching an inspiring vision while corrosive politics still rage produces cynicism, not energy, so you usually <em>stabilise the threat and friction first</em>. Fund them with conviction, keep a relational move alongside any structural one, and protect the de-load when the mid-quarter shock hits (a key engineer resigns, or a rival division demands the roadmap stay frozen).`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover, measure & defend to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Defend a 3-minute case to the PE board chair: your diagnosis, your two choices, and the 12-week trajectory. Show how you sustain the gain <strong>without permanent overtime</strong>, and how you monitor for the <em>acceleration trap</em> — a week-4 adrenaline spike that looks like energy while overtime and attrition climb. The board probes hard: is this <strong>durable productive energy</strong> or a short-lived spike that deepens burnout? Embed owners, watch energy and overtime together, and tell the honest 12-week story.`,

