                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_corrosive:     `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> High hours plus cynicism, blame and post-merger friction is corrosive energy — high intensity, negative quality. Now you can fix the relationships and autonomy the reorganisation broke, instead of treating tiredness as the disease.`,
                    coach_r1_burnout:       `<strong>Careful — you've called it simple burnout.</strong> But your data shows 9.2 overtime hours AND cynicism, blame and politics. That's high-intensity <em>negative</em> energy — corrosive, not exhaustion-from-passion. A yoga subscription on top of corrosive energy is a £20,000 apology. What's actually draining the 320 people?`,
                    coach_r1_resigned:      `<strong>Resigned inertia is low intensity — this isn't that.</strong> Your people are working 9.2 overtime hours; the intensity is high, not low. The problem is the negative quality of that intensity, not withdrawal. Re-read the energy as corrosive.`,
                    coach_r1_comfortable:   `<strong>Comfortable energy? Look again.</strong> Calm, complacent divisions don't post the highest hours and the steepest engagement drop in the company. This is high-intensity, negative-quality energy — the opposite of comfortable.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read both axes correctly — high intensity AND negative quality — and named it corrosive energy. That is exactly the lens that tells you to clear the friction before you sell a vision.`,
                    coach_r1_quality:       `<strong>You've misread the quality of the energy.</strong> Tired-but-pulling-together is positive quality; what your data shows is cynicism, blame and lost trust. The intensity is real, but its quality is negative — that's the whole point of the matrix.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You're reading hours as energy.</strong> Low willingness to judge the quality means you'll mistake frantic activity for productive energy. The hours are high; the question is whether that intensity is positive or corrosive.`,
                    coach_r2_productive:    `<strong>Productive energy is the right destination.</strong> High intensity AND positive quality — focused, joint effort. Now you need a route that converts the corrosive intensity you already have into positive quality, not one that simply dials everything down or pushes harder.`,
                    coach_r2_comfortable:   `<strong>Comfortable energy is a safe harbour, not the goal.</strong> Dialling the intensity right down relieves the burnout but risks complacency in a division that has to ship the core product. Aim for productive energy — keep the intensity, fix the quality.`,
                    coach_r2_harder:        `<strong>"Push harder" is how you deepen the trap.</strong> The division is already working the most hours in the company. More output without fixing the negative quality just accelerates burnout and attrition. Energy doesn't follow results when the energy is corrosive.`,
                    coach_r2_dragon:        `<strong>Slaying the dragon first fits corrosive energy.</strong> A shared threat mobilises fastest and channels the high intensity outward, clearing the internal friction. That earns you the right to sell a vision later — the princess sustains, the dragon stabilises.`,
                    coach_r2_princess:      `<strong>You've announced a bold vision to a room that doesn't trust each other.</strong> When energy is corrosive, an inspiring speech reads as more management noise — watch the cynicism rise. Slay the dragon first: what shared threat can you rally them against before you sell the dream?`,
                    coach_r2_both:          `<strong>Both at full volume is the acceleration trap forming.</strong> Vision plus threat plus everything-at-once is exactly the overload that masquerades as energy and burns the team out. Sequence them — you don't have the attention to do both well.`,
                    coach_r2_deload:        `<strong>De-loading is the move most teams skip.</strong> Taking work OFF the plate is what makes a rally sustainable rather than an adrenaline spike. With the dragon strategy and real de-loading, you channel intensity without piling on more.`,
                    coach_r2_addload:       `<strong>You're adding load to an already overloaded team.</strong> The week-4 pulse may spike — but overtime climbs and more people start interviewing. That's the acceleration trap: you mistook adrenaline for productive energy. What did you take OFF the plate?`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `<strong>Five levers, all personally sponsored?</strong> Your own diagnostic says leadership attention is the scarcest resource. Five half-sponsored initiatives signal "flavour of the month" — the team has seen this movie. Two done with conviction beat five that fade by week six.`,
                    coach_r3_perks:         `<strong>A wellness week won't touch corrosive energy.</strong> A meditation app on top of politics and friction is a £20,000 apology. Perks treat the symptom (exhaustion) and ignore the disease (negative quality). Spend on relationships, autonomy and load instead.`,
                    coach_r3_reorg:         `<strong>Another reorganisation? That's the third redraw in two years.</strong> Restructuring is exactly what created the corrosive energy. Boxes don't carry energy — people and relationships do. A fresh org chart re-opens the wounds you need to heal.`,
                    coach_r3_structureonly: `<strong>This is structure-only thinking.</strong> Energy is relational, not just structural — without a move that rebuilds trust, models the new behaviour or offers a vision, redrawing process won't shift the negative quality. Put a relational lever alongside the structural one.`,
                    coach_r3_sequenced:     `<strong>That is the sequence the matrix recommends.</strong> Stabilise the threat and friction first, then mobilise the vision second. You clear the corrosion, earn trust, and only then ask the team to follow you toward the princess — energy that builds rather than backfires.`,
                    coach_r3_threatfirst:   `<strong>Stabilising first is the right instinct.</strong> Clearing the friction before broadcasting a vision means the energy you raise has somewhere positive to go. Make sure the second lever is the one that mobilises people forward.`,
                    coach_r3_visionfirst:   `<strong>Vision first, politics later — watch the cynicism rise.</strong> You've inspired a room that still doesn't trust each other after the merger. They've heard inspiring speeches before. When energy is corrosive you slay the dragon first; the vision lands only once the friction is cleared.`,
                    coach_r3_parallel:      `<strong>Both at once is how the attention runs out.</strong> Twelve weeks isn't "no time to sequence" — it's exactly why sequencing matters. Doing everything in parallel splits your sponsorship and reads as another overloaded push.`,
                    coach_r3_visionlever:   `<strong>You launched the vision as the very first move.</strong> Selling the dream into unresolved politics is the classic mis-sequence — the cynicism rises before the energy does. Lead with the lever that clears the friction; bring the vision second.`,
                    coach_r3_underfund:     `<strong>You've under-funded the two levers.</strong> A token budget signals "flavour of the month" to a team that has seen initiatives fade. Back your two choices with real money and real sponsorship so they read as commitment, not gesture.`,
                    coach_r3_nodeload:      `<strong>You didn't protect the de-load when the shock hit.</strong> A key engineer resigned and you let the work pile back on. The rally becomes an adrenaline spike, overtime climbs, and you're back in the acceleration trap. Protect what you took off the plate.`,
                    coach_r4_embed:         `<strong>Embedding is how energy lasts.</strong> Protected autonomy, lighter meetings and named owners mean the gain survives once your 12 weeks of attention end — durable productive energy, not a project that decays the moment you look away.`,
                    coach_r4_rally:         `<strong>More rallying is more adrenaline, not more energy.</strong> Town halls and urgency hold the number up for a while, then overtime and attrition climb. That's the acceleration trap dressed as sustainment. What stays in place when the noise stops?`,
                    coach_r4_overtime:      `<strong>You can't bank durable energy on 9 overtime hours.</strong> High morale doesn't make chronic overload sustainable — it's exactly what drives the burnout and the regretted attrition. Sustainability means lower demands, not just higher spirits.`,
                    coach_r4_monitor:       `<strong>Watching energy and overtime together is the right instrument.</strong> A rising energy score with rising overtime is the acceleration trap's fingerprint — only by tracking both can you tell durable productive energy from an adrenaline spike.`,
                    coach_r4_energyonly:    `<strong>Energy-only monitoring will fool you.</strong> The score can spike on adrenaline while overtime and attrition climb underneath. Watch the leading load and people indicators too, or you'll declare victory on a spike that's about to collapse.`,
                    coach_r4_nomonitor:     `<strong>No live monitoring is how the last push died.</strong> A single week-12 number tells the board nothing about whether the energy lasts. Without leading indicators you can't see the acceleration trap until the team has already burned out.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest answer.</strong> Energy rising while overtime falls, with leading-indicator evidence and named owners — that's a trajectory that survives the handover, not a number that collapses the week the attention moves on.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>A week-4 spike is not a recovery.</strong> Declaring victory on an adrenaline bump is precisely the acceleration trap the board is probing for. The spin holds until the overtime and attrition data catch up — and they will, in front of the chair.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Eighteen months? The board reviews in 12 weeks.</strong> A beautiful long-term culture programme outsources the division before it starts. Name what can move a leading indicator now and tell the honest 12-week story.`,

